<Project>

  <!--
    Repo-wide MSBuild defaults. There is no .sln here and the five projects build
    independently, so this file is their only shared configuration point.

    Boundary: only properties that should be identical across all projects. Anything
    specific to a single project stays in its own csproj — don't turn this into a
    second dumping ground.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>

    <!-- Single source of truth for the product version.
         Before this, 0.1.6 was hand-copied into three csproj files (App / Cli / Avalonia),
         while Core and Checks carried no Version at all — the result was a single build
         artifact where Core.dll reported 1.0.0 and Cli.dll reported 0.1.6.
         installer/Build.ps1 and installer/Test.ps1 also read the version from this file;
         stop copying extra copies anywhere else. -->
    <Version>0.9.1</Version>

    <!-- The NuGet-side equivalent of the repo's existing "version + SHA-256 double pin"
         philosophy: pin the shipped dependency closure in packages.lock.json so different
         dev machines don't resolve different versions.

         Spell out the boundary, don't oversell it: what this fixes is **version drift**,
         not tampering — NuGet global packages already carry SHA-512 checks, and a lock
         file is not a signature.

         **Policy: one lock file is tracked, the dependency-chain root.**
           · AxmolHub (the app, shipped) — tracked. Measured shape: a net8.0 node of 8 Direct
             plus 60 Transitive plus 2 Project entries, then one 9-entry node per declared
             RuntimeIdentifiers (linux-x64, osx-arm64, osx-x64, win-x64).
           · AxmolHub.Agent — opted out in its own csproj and untracked. Common code that both
             App and Checks reference and that is never shipped alone, and NuGet's own wording
             is that a common code project's lock file has "little say over the packages
             resolved for projects that depend on it". Measured before deleting it: all 24
             package entries it carried already sat in the App file at identical versions.
           · AxmolHub.Checks — opted out and untracked for the same reason: 23 entries, not one
             of them Direct, every version arriving through Agent, and nothing shipped.
           · AxmolHub.Core / AxmolHub.Cli — opted out: no PackageReference at all, so a lock
             file would hold nothing but empty framework and RID nodes.
         A project that gains its first PackageReference re-decides by this rule rather than
         copying a version number: is what it builds shipped on its own? If not, opt out.

         Presence outranks the property, and this is the part that gets missed: NuGet consumes
         a packages.lock.json that exists in a project directory even when
         RestorePackagesWithLockFile is false, and while that file matches the declared
         dependencies it restores out of it with no re-evaluation of the graph at all. Opting
         out therefore stops NuGet from writing the file; it does not stop a leftover copy from
         pinning. Delete any that reappears in an opted-out directory. The two that used to be
         tracked are named in .gitignore, so git status will not show such a leftover.

         RestoreLockedMode is deliberately not enabled, and "not enabled" means not enforced,
         not unused: the tracked file already decides which versions every restore on this
         machine and in CI resolves. Locked mode would add one guarantee, that a mismatch
         fails the restore instead of quietly rewriting the file. Two facts keep that a separate
         decision, and neither is the SDK — build.yml runs DOTNET_SDK_VERSION 10.0.x, the same
         major as a dev machine:
           · shape. A committed multi-node file does not match the base-plus-one-RID graph that
             a publish carrying a RID writes, so enabling locked mode today would fail the four
             package jobs until the packaging restore path is reworked.
           · the .NET SDK injects implicit PackageReference items, so a different SDK patch can
             change the graph and fail a locked restore; the documented mitigation is a
             global.json, which this repo deliberately does not pin.
         The order is unchanged: keep the tracked file honest, then decide whether to enforce it.

         **Lock files jitter, and that is deterministic NuGet behavior, not an environment
         anomaly** (re-measured on 2026-10-11 against this tree). NuGet rewrites a lock file only
         when its check on the declared dependencies says the file is stale, so an ordinary
         publish with nothing bumped leaves the tracked file byte-identical. When it does rewrite,
         the shape depends on whether the restore passed a RID:
           · without a RID: the base TFM node plus one node per declared RuntimeIdentifiers
             (5 nodes for the app);
           · with a RID: the base node plus that single RID node (2 nodes for the app). It reaches
             the referenced projects on the way too, which is what bolted a stray net8.0/win-x64
             node onto Agent's file in the middle of the installer change committed as c4564e9.
             No referenced project writes a lock any more, so that reach has nothing left to land
             on — until a command-line property turns generation back on: measured, one restore
             with the property forced on brought both Agent's and Core's file back.
         So the day a package version is bumped is the day a packaging run leaves a pruned file.
         installer/Build.ps1 now follows its publish with a RID-less restore of the app project,
         which rewrites the committed shape before any later step of the run can fail. A manual
         publish carrying a RID still needs that restore by hand; see docs/building-from-source.md.
         Do not cure this by suppressing generation during a publish: the file is read whether or
         not it is written, and an unmaintained pin is worse than a dirty one. -->
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>

  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
